icle\"\n    # Use get_by_role to find all issue elements\n    issue_elements = page.get_by_role(\"listitem\").or_(page.get_by_role(\"article\"))\n    issues_list = []\n    count = await issue_elements.count()\n    for i in range(count):\n        issue_text = await issue_elements.nth(i).text_content()\n        issues_list.append(issue_text)\n    \n    # Display the list of issues\n    for issue in issues_list:\n        print(issue)\n    \n    # Return the list as a string for termination\n    return '\\n'.join(issues_list)\n</code>\n\n<exception>\n'SyntaxError': unterminated string literal (detected at line 1178)\n</exception>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\nI need to display the list of issues in the 'umano/AndroidSlidingUpPanel' repository that have labels related to BUG. I'm currently on the GitLab dashboard page, as shown in the accessibility tree. The previous code attempted to navigate to the project details page using the knowledge base function 'navigate_to_project_details', but there was a syntax error in the previous execution. I must start fresh from the current state. First, I need to navigate to the project details page for 'umano/AndroidSlidingUpPanel'. The knowledge base function 'navigate_to_project_details' is available and designed for this exact purpose, so I should use it. After navigating, I'll be on the project details page. Then, I need to go to the issues page for that project. From the accessibility tree, I can see that on the dashboard, each project has a link for issues (e.g., '41' for a11yproject.com). On the project details page, I expect a similar link. I'll click the 'Issues' link. Then, I need to filter issues by BUG labels. I'll look for a filter input (likely a searchbox) and set it to filter by label:BUG. After applying the filter, I need to retrieve and display the list of issues. I must use accessibility tree-centric selectors, so I'll use page.get_by_role() and related methods. I'll avoid CSS selectors. I'll ensure to wait for page loads 